    "lawCode": "Add §13.12, amd §13.10, Pks & Rec L",
    "lawSection": "Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S809A\n\nSPONSOR: SERRANO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the parks, recreation and historic preservation law, in\nrelation to prohibiting littering and dumping on state park lands\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo create a deterrent to littering and dumping in State Parks and\nHistoric Sites.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one will add a new Section 13.12 to the parks, recreation and\nhistoric preservation law to prohibit littering on park lands. This bill\nwould make exemptions for reasonable use of ashes, sand, salt or other\nmaterial for the purpose of reducing the hazard of or providing traction\non snow, ice or sleet. Additionally, it would create penalties for\nlittering in State parks.\n\nSection two of this bill would amend Section 13.10 of the parks, recre-\nation and historic preservation law to make clarifying changes that this\nsection of law applies to dumping in State Parks. It will also increase\nthe penalties for dumping.\n\nSection three provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York's State Parks are among the most magnificent in the Nation.  By\nenacting stringent policies to protect our parks, we will deter litter-\ning and dumping to ensure our park staff spends less time picking up\ntrash and more time maintaining and beautifying parks in other ways.\nMoreover, in the event that someone does litter, a punishment of commu-\nnity service will help maintain the tarnished park. A more serious\npenalty should if apply for someone who dumps rubbish or waste in our\nstate parks.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nPrevious Similar Bills:\n\n2021-2022: S.1405 - Passed Senate\n\n2019-2020: S.2494 - Passed Senate 61-0\n\n2017-2018: S.3053 - Passed Senate 62-0\n\n2015-2016: S.956-, Passed Senate 62-0\n\n2014: S.3014 - Passed Senate 57-0\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThis bill will have no fiscal impact on the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act would take effect immediately.\n",
